{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed56627177/3/codegen_prompts/1.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed56627177/3`\n- 题号：`1`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed56627177/3/1.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"1\">\n- `description`：根据彩色锚点之间的围栏结构，识别每段围栏围住的区域，并用锚点颜色的配对规则填充该区域。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为黑色（black）。\n  - 网格中散布若干单像素的彩色锚点（如 red、blue、green 等），每个颜色恰好出现两个。\n  - 同色的两个锚点之间由灰色（grey）像素组成的直线段或折线段相连，形成\"围栏\"。\n  - 多条围栏可能共享灰色像素段，整体围栏网络将网格划分为若干封闭区域。\n  - 每个封闭区域的边界恰好由两条不同颜色对的围栏段构成（即该区域被两对锚点的围栏共同围住）。\n  - 网格的某个角落有一个小型颜色配对图例——一个 N×3 的小表格，每行前两格各放一种锚点颜色，第三格放结果颜色，表示这两种锚点颜色的围栏共同围住的区域应填充的颜色。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步：识别每个被围栏完全封闭的区域（不含围栏像素本身）。\n  - 第二步：确定围住该区域的两条围栏分别属于哪两对锚点颜色，例如 red 对和 blue 对。\n  - 第三步：查找角落图例中与该两色组合匹配的行，取其结果颜色。\n  - 第四步：用该结果颜色填充整个封闭区域的背景像素。围栏像素、锚点像素和图例像素保持不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要先理解灰色围栏将空间分区，再识别每个区域由哪两对颜色的围栏界定，最后通过角落的颜色配对图例查表得到填充色——三层推理缺一不可。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/dbff022c, train/9f669b64, train/2ccd9fef, train/465b7d93, train/5adee1b2, eval/88e364bc, eval/221dfab4, train/3bd292e8\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3095\nmechanism=0.3634\nlexical=0.0120\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:grid_edge, object:template, operation:flood_fill, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/dbff022c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Use a color palette legend on the grid edge to fill sprites' interiors based on their border color mapping.\",\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"hollow sprites with colored borders s\",\n  \"dst\": \"interior of same sprite d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d.fill_color == palette_lookup(s.border_color)\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill d with palette[s.border_color]\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"palette is 2xN or Nx2 block on grid edge; read direction based on edge position: top edge reads top-to-bottom, bottom edge reads bottom-to-top, left edge reads left-to-right; first row/column is border color, second is fill color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: locate 2xN or Nx2 palette legend on grid edge; Step 2: build color mapping (border -> fill) based on reading direction; Step 3: for each hollow sprite, lookup border color in palette and fill interior with corresponding fill color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"color-mapping\",\n    \"lookup-table\",\n    \"flood-fill\",\n    \"border-detection\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The challenge is correctly reading the palette legend based on its edge position and orientation\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f669b64\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2394\nmechanism=0.2602\nlexical=0.0123\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:grid_edge, object:line_segment, output:same_size, relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9f669b64.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/9f669b64.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nPush the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one\n  horizontal or vertical axis.\n- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.\n- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular\n  to the movement axis; this is the target to split.\n\nRule\n- Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is\n  flush with the grid edge in that direction.\n- Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.\n- Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and\n  shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.\n- Keep the other end object unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels\n  away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"middle solid rectangular object between two end objects\",\n  \"path\": \"straight horizontal or vertical line away from the non-target end object and through the perpendicular solid target rectangle/bar\",\n  \"dst\": \"place flush with the grid edge beyond the target\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"paint only the final moved rectangle at dst\",\n  \"transform\": \"erase original source pixels; split the target rectangle/bar along the moving object's two-cell-wide path and shift its two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement; keep the other end object unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2ccd9fef\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2365\nmechanism=0.2685\nlexical=0.0125\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:frame, object:template, operation:shape_match, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/2ccd9fef.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/2ccd9fef.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nPredict the next panel in a framed sequence by continuing each scheduled colored interval.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is made from three or more equal panels in one row or one column.\n- The final input panel is the static target template; every earlier panel differs from it only by dynamic colors absent from the template.\n- Each dynamic color appears in a regular temporal schedule across the earlier panels.\n\nRule\n- Keep the final template panel as the output canvas.\n- For every dynamic color whose schedule reaches the next panel, compare its last two appearances.\n- Extrapolate each one-dimensional interval along the color's changing row or column axis and paint that predicted mask.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for the last visible panel, but for the next frame after the blank/template slot.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"sequence of equal rectangular panels ending with a blank/static template panel\",\n  \"dst\": \"next panel in the sequence\",\n  \"transform\": \"use the template panel as the output canvas; for each dynamic color absent from the template, follow its regular panel schedule and extrapolate the last two interval changes one more step along the changing row or column axis\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same shape as one panel\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"465b7d93\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2393\nmechanism=0.2725\nlexical=0.0126\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:line_segment, relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/465b7d93.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/465b7d93.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove the small colored sprite into the hollow frame by stretching its shape to the frame interior.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular\n  one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it.\n- The frame interior is initially background.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite from its outside location.\n- Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to\n  interior extremes.\n- Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of\n  adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside\n  those drawn segments.\n\nKey insight:\nThe outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior,\nnot a bitmap to scale by block replication.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"small colored sprite s outside the frame\",\n  \"dst\": \"hollow rectangular frame interior d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"unique s maps to unique d; s.bbox extremes align to d.bbox extremes\",\n  \"transform\": \"clear s; stretch s into d by mapping bbox extremes, drawing 4-adjacent segments, and filling enclosed regions\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5adee1b2\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2383\nmechanism=0.2879\nlexical=0.0127\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
